  "ai_language_tip": "Speak clear Icelandic (is) if possible, but English is spoken by almost everyone and many service staff are non-Icelandic, so English is often the practical default; Polish is common among workers.",
  "english_ok": "yes — Near-universal English; tourism staff often speak English better than Icelandic.",
  "politeness": "Very informal: first names and 'þú' with everyone; open with 'Góðan dag' or 'Halló' and say whom you call for; close with 'Takk fyrir, bless'.",
  "name_tip": "Icelanders use first names and patronymics (-son / -dóttir), not family surnames, and are listed by first name; give the first name clearly and spell letters like þ, ð, æ.",
  "phone_tip": "All numbers are 7 digits with no trunk 0 (e.g. 123 4567), +354; mobiles usually start with 6, 7 or 8.",
  "time_tip": "TRAP: 'hálf átta' = 7:30 (half to eight); 'korter í átta' = 7:45; read back in 24h.",
  "number_tip": "Numbers 1–4 change by gender ('tveir/tvær/tvö'); 'íbúð 0302' is a flat number (floor 3, flat 2), not a count; prices in ISK are large numbers, so read them in thousands.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid lunch 11:30–13:00, dinner peak 18:00–20:30, calls after 20:00 and weekends for offices; summer tourist season fills tours and guesthouses.",
  "closed_days": "Shops open on Sundays in Reykjavík; public holidays include Maundy Thursday, Good Friday, Easter Monday, Sumardagurinn fyrsti (first day of summer in April), 1 May, 17 June, Frídagur verslunarmanna (first Monday in August), 24–26 and 31 December.",
